/*!
    \page configuration.html
    \title Configuration
    \previouspage Working with a Server
    \nextpage Architecture

    This chapter describes what the application stores, where it stores it, and the
    settings that affect its behavior. The location policy is implemented once in
    \l AppPaths (see \l {Architecture}).

    \section1 Installed versus portable

    The application runs in one of two modes, decided once at startup:

    \list
        \li \b Installed — binaries live in the read-only install directory, while user
            data (settings, logs, cache, PKI, and the writable database) lives in the
            per-user Windows locations.
        \li \b Portable — when a \c portable.ini marker sits next to the executable, all
            user data lives under the application directory, so the whole installation
            can be moved together with its data.
    \endlist

    Read-only material bundled next to the executable (the seed database and the PKI
    skeleton) is copied into writable storage on first run, so the program works even
    when its install directory is not writable.

    \section1 Application settings

    General settings are kept in an INI store. These include the last connection, the
    pinned focus node, the recent-projects list, and the selected UI language.

    \section1 Client projects (\c .uaproj)

    A client project stores the connection configuration and the set of monitored nodes
    so a session can be restored later (\l ProjectData). Projects are JSON files written
    atomically. Recent projects are remembered in the application settings rather than in
    the project file.

    \section1 Server projects (\c .uaserver)

    A server project stores the full server definition — namespaces, nodes, enumeration
    types, endpoint, and security (\l{ServerProject}{ServerProject::ProjectData}). It is
    a JSON file written atomically by its serializer.

    \section1 Monitored-node database

    Monitored variables are persisted in a SQLite database (\l NodeDatabase), seeded from
    a bundled default on first run and never overwritten on later upgrades, so your data
    survives updates.

    \section1 Security material (PKI)

    Certificate-based client security and server security each use a PKI store under the
    writable data directory. For a designed server, a self-signed certificate is created
    in the server's own store when you enable secure endpoints.

    \section1 Language

    The UI language (English, German, French, Italian, Russian, Ukrainian) is applied at startup and can
    be switched at runtime; the choice is saved in the application settings
    (\l LocaleController).
*/
